# Implementation Plan - Phase 2: Face Preprocessing

This plan details the design, architecture, and validation strategy for **Phase 2: Face Preprocessing**. 

The goal of this phase is to construct an offline, low-latency preprocessing pipeline to transform raw $112 \times 112$ face crops into mathematical inputs (tensors) that maximize facial recognition accuracy under changing lighting conditions (e.g., shadows or low light).

---

## Why Preprocessing Matters & Its Impact on Accuracy

Deep learning face recognition models like **MobileFaceNet** are highly sensitive to domain shifts. Standardizing inputs is critical for two reasons:
1. **Lighting & Contrast Invariance**: Real-world environments have diverse lighting. Adaptive equalization (using **CLAHE**) standardizes contrast and brightness without introducing noise, helping the model extract structural facial features (like nose bridges and eye geometry) rather than getting confused by shadows.
2. **Numerical Alignment**: Models are trained on standard ranges (typically normalized to `[-1, 1]`, color-ordered in `RGB`, and structured as `NCHW` tensors). Feeding raw BGR `[0, 255]` pixel ranges will result in completely random and invalid embeddings, destroying matching accuracy.

---

## User Review Required

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Normalization Parameters**: MobileFaceNet standard models typically expect pixels normalized using the formula:
> $$\text{Normalized} = \frac{\text{Pixel} - 127.5}{128.0}$$
> This maps the `[0, 255]` range to `[-0.99609, 0.99609]`. We will implement this as the default mode, but make it configurable to support standard `[0.0, 1.0]` or standard Z-score division if other models are integrated.

